/**
 * @mainpage wlonvif C++ API
 *
 * @section overview Overview
 *
 * `wlonvif` is a C++20 client library for selected ONVIF Device, Media1, PTZ,
 * Imaging, Analytics, PullPoint Event, and discovery workflows. It favors a compact typed API,
 * explicit resource bounds, deterministic lifecycle behavior, and testability over
 * generated bindings for the complete ONVIF schema family.
 *
 * The library provides:
 *
 * - Device identity, services, and capabilities; clock, NTP, and scope configuration;
 * - Media1 profiles and their full configurations, video encoder options and changes,
 *   stream and snapshot URIs, and bounded downloads;
 * - normalized PTZ spaces, status, movement, Stop, emergency Stop, presets, preset
 *   tours, and image orientation;
 * - Imaging settings within the device's ranges, and Analytics modules and rules;
 * - a stateful manual PTZ controller and calibrated viewport movement mapping;
 * - IPv4 WS-Discovery plus bounded targeted and CIDR direct probing;
 * - PullPoint subscription management, bounded event queues, and continuity health;
 * - challenge-driven HTTP and WS-Security authentication, with WS-Security
 *   timestamps corrected for the device's clock offset;
 * - endpoint and resolved-address authorization boundaries;
 * - bounded SOAP/XML parsing and structured errors; and
 * - DeviceSession::call() for operations without a typed call; and
 * - an in-process virtual Device/Media/PTZ/Imaging/Analytics/Event backend for tests.
 *
 * This is not a Media2, Recording, Replay, codec, RTP/RTSP playback, general ONVIF
 * server, or conformance-certification implementation.
 * Cameras differ substantially by model and firmware, so deployments must qualify
 * the exact operations they depend on.
 *
 * @section architecture Architecture
 *
 * Applications normally assemble the client in four layers:
 *
 * @verbatim
 * application / UI / service
 *          |
 *      DeviceSession  ---- lifecycle, credentials, child cancellation, PTZ safety
 *       /    |    \
 *  Device  Media1   PTZ / Imaging / Analytics / Events
 *          |
 * HttpTransport ---- EndpointPolicy ---- optional CandidateAuthorizer
 *          |
 *       Runtime  ---- completion dispatch and bounded shutdown participation
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * Runtime owns completion dispatch. HttpTransport performs HTTP(S) work.
 * EndpointPolicy authorizes advertised URLs before requests are constructed.
 * CandidateAuthorizer, when configured through CurlTransportOptions, authorizes
 * the resolved address set immediately before connection. DeviceSession owns the
 * connected Device endpoint and creates MediaClient, PtzClient, and
 * ManagedEventStream objects that share its backend and cancellation state.
 *
 * The public header is:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include <wlonvif/wlonvif.hpp>
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section getting_started Connecting a session
 *
 * Prefer DeviceSessionOptions for new code because it groups validated ownership,
 * policy, credential, and call-lifetime inputs:
 *
 * @code{.cpp}
 * using namespace std::chrono_literals;
 *
 * auto created = wlonvif::Runtime::create();
 * if (!created) {
 *     // Inspect created.error().category and created.error().message.
 *     return;
 * }
 * auto runtime = created.value();
 *
 * wlonvif::CurlTransportOptions transport_options;
 * transport_options.runtime = runtime;
 * transport_options.limits = wlonvif::XmlLimits{};
 * auto transport = wlonvif::make_curl_transport(std::move(transport_options));
 *
 * wlonvif::DeviceSessionOptions session_options;
 * session_options.device_url = "https://camera/onvif/device_service";
 * session_options.transport = std::move(transport);
 * session_options.endpoint_policy = wlonvif::make_same_host_policy();
 * session_options.runtime = runtime;
 * session_options.credential_provider =
 *     wlonvif::make_fixed_credential_provider({"operator", "password", false});
 * session_options.call = {
 *     std::chrono::steady_clock::now() + 10s,
 *     wlonvif::CancellationSource{}.token()
 * };
 *
 * auto connection = wlonvif::DeviceSession::connect(std::move(session_options));
 * std::move(connection).start([](auto result) {
 *     if (!result) {
 *         // Use result.error().category for control flow.
 *         return;
 *     }
 *     auto session = result.value();
 *     // Retain session and consume its asynchronous operations.
 * });
 * @endcode
 *
 * A Task is cold: merely constructing it performs no work. It is move-only and
 * single-consumer; start it exactly once with `std::move(task).start(completion)`.
 * A completion may be dispatched by Runtime or run inline if shutdown has already
 * stopped queue admission. Applications that require a particular UI or event-loop
 * thread should marshal the completion through their own executor.
 *
 * @section result_model Results, deadlines, and cancellation
 *
 * Every asynchronous public operation returns `Task<Result<T>>`.
 *
 * - Test Result before accessing `value()` or `error()`.
 * - Use Error::category and Error::operation for program logic. Error::message is
 *   owned, redacted diagnostic prose and is not a stable protocol.
 * - CallOptions::deadline is an absolute `std::chrono::steady_clock` time point.
 * - CancellationSource owns cancellation state; CancellationToken is a cheap,
 *   copyable observation view.
 * - DeviceSession links each caller token with its session cancellation source.
 * - Caller-visible DNS resolution, candidate authorization, HTTP, SOAP, and
 *   service work observe cancellation and deadlines.
 *
 * Cancellation cannot retract a request already accepted by a device. Error and
 * MoveResult therefore preserve uncertain-delivery information. Do not blindly
 * retry a non-idempotent PTZ movement after timeout or cancellation.
 *
 * Error optionally owns typed HttpErrorDetail, SoapFaultDetail,
 * HostPermissionDetail, and SafetyActionDetail records. SOAP detail strings are
 * bounded. HTTP 401, 403, and 429 and recognized SOAP busy/rate-limit signals are
 * classified without requiring callers to parse diagnostic strings.
 *
 * @section lifecycle Session and runtime lifecycle
 *
 * DeviceSession is thread-safe and follows one authoritative state machine:
 *
 * @verbatim
 * open  ->  closing  ->  closed
 * @endverbatim
 *
 * DeviceSession::close() is idempotent. It rejects new operations, requests child
 * cancellation, closes the credential provider without holding session state
 * locks, runs bounded PTZ safety cleanup, and settles all waiters once active native
 * accounting completes. CloseResult reports whether cleanup and safety completed
 * inside the caller's budget. A budget expiry does not abandon internal accounting
 * or reopen the session.
 *
 * Consume `close()` whenever the final result matters. Destruction is a
 * non-blocking cancellation and resource-release backstop. Runtime::shutdown()
 * stops new completion admission, invokes registered bounded cleanup, drains
 * accepted work, and terminates its worker safely. It is idempotent and safe when
 * called concurrently or from a runtime completion.
 *
 * @section security Transport and credential security
 *
 * Endpoint authorization has two distinct layers:
 *
 * 1. EndpointPolicy checks an advertised URL relative to the original Device URL.
 *    `make_same_host_policy()` accepts HTTP(S) service endpoints on another port of
 *    the same host. `make_same_origin_policy()` also requires the original port.
 *    Both reject user-info and unrelated hosts.
 * 2. CandidateAuthorizer receives the normalized hostname and port, complete
 *    resolved-address set, proposed address, original/effective URLs, endpoint
 *    purpose and provenance, operation metadata, and an opaque attempt ID. The
 *    transport connects only after approval and pins the approved address with
 *    libcurl to prevent an unchecked second resolution.
 *
 * DNS candidate resolution observes the request deadline and cancellation token.
 * Redirect following is disabled. HTTPS certificate and hostname verification are
 * left enabled. Challenge parsing is token-aware: the transport sends no HTTP
 * credentials until the server advertises a supported authentication scheme.
 * HTTP Basic over unencrypted HTTP requires explicit
 * Credentials::allow_cleartext_basic opt-in.
 *
 * CredentialProvider supplies independent, move-only CredentialLease values.
 * Provider close rejects new acquisition and best-effort erases provider buffers;
 * lease destruction best-effort erases lease buffers. Erasure is not a forensic
 * guarantee because allocators, TLS libraries, operating systems, or devices may
 * retain copies outside the API's ownership.
 *
 * XmlLimits bounds HTTP body bytes, XML depth, nodes, attributes, and cumulative
 * text. Binary downloads add their own caller-selected byte bound. Discovery and
 * event queues, discovery host/concurrency counts, authentication headers, and the
 * optional PTZ web protocol are bounded independently.
 *
 * @section device_services Device, Media, PTZ, and Events
 *
 * A connected DeviceSession directly exposes Device operations:
 *
 * - DeviceSession::get_device_information()
 * - DeviceSession::get_system_date_and_time()
 * - DeviceSession::get_scopes()
 * - DeviceSession::get_services()
 * - DeviceSession::get_capabilities()
 * - DeviceSession::set_system_date_and_time(), DeviceSession::get_ntp(), and
 *   DeviceSession::set_ntp()
 * - DeviceSession::get_scope_entries(), DeviceSession::set_scopes(),
 *   DeviceSession::add_scopes(), and DeviceSession::remove_scopes()
 * - DeviceSession::get_hostname(), DeviceSession::get_network_interfaces(),
 *   DeviceSession::get_dns(), DeviceSession::get_discovery_mode(),
 *   DeviceSession::get_users(), DeviceSession::system_reboot(), and their setters
 * - DeviceSession::call() for any other operation
 *
 * DeviceSession::imaging() returns ImagingClient for brightness, contrast, and
 * similar settings; DeviceSession::analytics() returns AnalyticsClient for
 * detection modules and rules. EventClient::get_topics() lists event topics, and
 * PullPointSubscriptionOptions::topics filters a subscription on the device.
 *
 * DeviceSession::media() returns MediaClient for Media1 profiles and stream or
 * snapshot URIs. `download_binary()` retrieves authorized bounded opaque content;
 * it does not decode images.
 *
 * DeviceSession::ptz() returns PtzClient. Public positions, translations, and
 * velocities use normalized `[-1,+1]` coordinates and retain the device's native
 * advertised space metadata in PtzSpaces. Available operations include continuous,
 * relative, and absolute movement, status, ordinary Stop, urgent emergency Stop,
 * presets (list, go to, save, remove), preset tours (create, modify, start, pause,
 * stop, remove), and image orientation (EFlip and Reverse).
 *
 * A service the device does not advertise is reported as ErrorCategory::unsupported
 * when its client is used, so applications can hide features a camera lacks.
 *
 * DeviceSession::event_client() returns EventClient. `create_pullpoint()` exposes
 * explicit create, pull, renew, unsubscribe, state, and bounded-close operations on
 * an independent PullPointSubscription. `open_managed_stream()` starts a polling
 * worker which renews before expiry, reconnects with bounded exponential backoff,
 * and reports possible continuity loss in band. Its local queue is bounded and
 * reports overflow with StreamHealth::events_dropped. Consume `close()` to stop the
 * worker and unsubscribe deterministically. DeviceSession::events() remains the
 * manual polling interface for compatibility.
 *
 * @section ptz_control Stateful and viewport PTZ control
 *
 * PtzController turns non-blocking manual input into bounded-rate PtzClient calls.
 * Its validated PtzControllerConfig controls ownership leases, dead zone and
 * response curves, host or firmware ramping, command interval and refresh,
 * minimum effective speed, maximum run duration, zoom-aware speed, soft limits,
 * keep-out zones, and status polling. It coalesces input to the latest intent and
 * retains no unbounded command queue. Focus loss, stale ownership, explicit Stop,
 * close, or watchdog expiry converge on an urgent Stop.
 *
 * ViewportMapper converts validated click or drag geometry into conservative
 * relative movement. ViewportCalibration supports content cropping, rotation,
 * mirroring, bounded translation, calibration age, direction-specific gains, and
 * interpolation across normalized zoom samples. PtzSettlingDetector provides a
 * small deterministic arrival-state helper for automatic operations.
 *
 * DeviceSession also maintains a PTZ movement safety ledger by profile. Accepted or
 * uncertain movement remains tracked until a successful applicable Stop or reliable
 * stopped status clears it. Session and runtime shutdown perform a bounded Stop
 * sweep; a failed safety Stop is retained structurally rather than replacing the
 * initiating operation's result.
 *
 * @section discovery Discovery
 *
 * `discover_devices()` combines configured discovery strategies into bounded,
 * deduplicated DeviceCandidate results. NetworkDiscoveryOptions controls multicast
 * interfaces, targeted unicast, direct common-path probing, explicit CIDRs,
 * concurrency, host limits, result limits, probe types, and progress reporting.
 * `local_ipv4_networks()` returns eligible directly connected IPv4 networks subject
 * to a host bound.
 *
 * DiscoveryAuthorizer is the pre-socket permission boundary for interface
 * enumeration, UDP binds and sends, explicit CIDR scans, and direct TCP targets.
 * Enable NetworkDiscoveryOptions::require_authorization in restricted hosts so a
 * missing authorizer fails with host_permission_denied before socket creation.
 *
 * Discovery evidence records how each candidate was observed. Multicast depends on
 * interface, firewall, routing, and camera behavior; known targets and explicitly
 * authorized CIDR scans are important fallbacks. Only scan networks for which the
 * operator has permission.
 *
 * DiscoverySession is a deterministic bounded observation queue used by tests and
 * adapters. It is not the network discovery entry point; use `discover_devices()`
 * for batch network discovery. LibraryFeatures::discovery_streaming describes a
 * separate embedding contract and is not a claim that batch discovery is absent.
 *
 * @section virtual_device Deterministic testing
 *
 * VirtualDeviceTransport implements HttpTransport and ServiceBackend without
 * opening sockets. Tests can configure identity, scopes, services, capabilities,
 * Media profiles and URIs, PTZ spaces and presets, artificial delay, forced errors,
 * and Event emission. It is intended for unit tests and application development,
 * not as a network-visible ONVIF server or conformance simulator.
 *
 * The project test matrix covers static and shared builds, warnings-as-errors,
 * AddressSanitizer/UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, ThreadSanitizer, install/package
 * consumption, XML fuzz smoke tests, and optional web-protocol fuzzing. Ordinary
 * tests are hardware-independent; real-device behavior requires an explicit smoke
 * or qualification run.
 *
 * @section build Linking and packaging
 *
 * The installed CMake package exports:
 *
 * - `wlonvif::core` for runtime, task/result, XML/SOAP, security, and shared types;
 * - `wlonvif::client` for HTTP transport, discovery, service clients, PTZ control,
 *   and viewport mapping; and
 * - `wlonvif::device` for VirtualDeviceTransport.
 *
 * @code{.cmake}
 * find_package(wlonvif 0.1 CONFIG REQUIRED)
 * add_executable(camera_app main.cpp)
 * target_link_libraries(camera_app PRIVATE wlonvif::client)
 * target_compile_features(camera_app PRIVATE cxx_std_20)
 * @endcode
 *
 * Core requirements are CMake 3.24, C++20, libcurl 8, libxml2 2.9, OpenSSL 3,
 * and platform threads. SDL3, Boost.Beast, and FFmpeg belong only to optional
 * examples and are not dependencies of the installed client libraries.
 *
 * `library_version()` returns the native semantic version. `PROJECT.txt` is the
 * single source of version information: CMake reads it for `project()`, the
 * package version file, and the generated `<wlonvif/version.hpp>` macros. `library_features()`
 * exposes embedding-contract readiness for candidate authorization, credential
 * providers, deterministic close, PTZ safety, PullPoint integration, and streaming
 * discovery. These flags are intended for adapters that must reject an incompatible
 * native library at startup; some flags describe a stricter adapter contract than
 * the presence of lower-level API operations.
 *
 * @section examples Examples and further guidance
 *
 * Build dependency-light examples with `WLONVIF_BUILD_EXAMPLES=ON`. They cover
 * discovery, Device inspection and configuration, inventory, endpoint policy,
 * virtual-device usage, Media profiles, URIs, snapshots, and encoders, Imaging,
 * Analytics, raw requests, PTZ diagnostics, PullPoint monitoring, camera health,
 * and bounded multi-camera inspection.
 *
 * `WLONVIF_BUILD_EXPLORER=ON` adds `wlonvif-explorer`, a browser application that
 * scans the network, signs in to a camera, and exposes every feature above, plus a
 * read-only compatibility check whose report can be attached to a bug report.
 * Controls for features a camera does not offer are dimmed, with the reason.
 *
 * `WLONVIF_BUILD_PTZ_CONTROL=ON` adds the SDL3 native keyboard/gamepad frontend.
 * `WLONVIF_BUILD_PTZ_WEB=ON` adds the loopback-first Boost.Beast browser frontend.
 * Both keep movement policy in PtzController and require explicit confirmation for
 * live movement. The browser example additionally documents its authenticated
 * session protocol, TLS/Origin boundary, optional FFmpeg MJPEG bridge, calibrated
 * viewport movement, and orderly interruption of stalled clients.
 *
 * Consult the repository documents for operational detail:
 *
 * - `README.md` — build options, all command-line examples, installation, real
 *   device smoke testing, comparisons, and deployment cautions;
 * - [Session and transport security](session-security.md) — lifecycle, transport,
 *   credential, error, and PTZ safety contracts;
 * - [Native PTZ control](ptz-control.md) — SDL mapping, calibration, safety, and
 *   qualification; and
 * - [Browser PTZ control](ptz-web.md) — browser protocol, FFmpeg bridge, calibration,
 *   remote threat model, and limitations.
 *
 * These three guides are included in this documentation as related pages.
 */
